R926: MAME 0.289 with the i960's and the TGP's clocks set from the environment
(Sega Model 2 core for MiSTer FPGA, study R926). M2_I960_CLK=<Hz> (MAME: 25 MHz)
and M2_TGP_CLK=<Hz> (MAME: 50 MHz, a third of it an instruction) are applied in
machine_start; without them MAME runs as before. What it showed: Sega Rally
picks each frame's AI-car detail from the time left after its first piece of
work (timer 2 at 0x5bade8), so a slower i960 demotes the cars nearest the
player -- at 10 MHz the third car flickers between the course line and its
physics frame to frame, as in the whole-core bench.
Apply in a MAME 0.289 tree: patch -p1 < mame_m2_clock.patch, then e.g.
make SUBTARGET=m2lod SOURCES=src/mame/sega/model2.cpp (17 s incremental). The
patch is MAME code (BSD-3-Clause, model2.cpp's own licence); a tool, not part
of the core.

diff --git a/src/mame/sega/model2.cpp b/src/mame/sega/model2.cpp
index 899828e6..2c45399d 100644
--- a/src/mame/sega/model2.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/sega/model2.cpp
@@ -158,6 +158,8 @@ TIMER_CALLBACK_MEMBER(model2_state::irq_mask_delayed_update)
 
 void model2_state::machine_start()
 {
+	if (const char *ck = getenv("M2_I960_CLK")) { m_maincpu->set_unscaled_clock(strtoul(ck, nullptr, 0)); osd_printf_info("R926: i960 clock %u\n", unsigned(m_maincpu->unscaled_clock())); }
+	if (const char *ck = getenv("M2_TGP_CLK")) { if (device_t *t = subdevice("copro_tgp")) t->set_unscaled_clock(strtoul(ck, nullptr, 0)); }
 	// initialize custom debugger pool, @see machine/model2.cpp
 	debug_init();
 
